# shir_nodes/field_strip.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# The cursor advance behind `${s#*D}`: the ABSOLUTE byte offset just past
# the first `delim` at/after `off` — or `off` UNCHANGED when no `delim`
# follows it (bash's strip is a no-op when the pattern does not match,
# so the cursor must not move). The result is a decimal STRING (the same
# channel every shell scalar number travels), consumed by the companion
# FieldGrab reads of the same cursor var. Emitted by `str-chop-cursor`.
#
# Proved: docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Field.thy.
#   spec: field_strip_no_move_on_miss — a non-match leaves the cursor at `off`,
#         and the paired grab then reads the whole remaining tail.
#   With it: field_strip_gt_off_on_hit and field_strip_progress (every hit
#   consumes at least one byte, so the converted loop terminates),
#   field_strip_le_length (the new offset stays inside the text) and
#   field_strip_stuck_iff_miss (the cursor does not move exactly on a miss —
#   the case this node must not be looped on, which is what FieldStripEnd and
#   the guarded pair exist for).
#   The arm does not clamp `off`: `off <= strlen(text)` is the transform's
#   read law (init 0, only these two nodes advance), stated as a hypothesis in
#   the theory, not a property of the node.
#
# Backends: C (memchr-free strchr-at-offset; target-gated offer).
# Fallback: sh2.fieldStrip(text, off, delim)

node FieldStrip
tag "FieldStrip"
kind expr
field text: expr
field off: expr
field delim: string
spec: TextOps#field_strip_no_move_on_miss
